JVM (Java Virtual Machine)
The JVM is the engine that actually runs Java programs.
It reads the compiled Java code (bytecode) and executes it on the machine.
The JVM also handles memory management, security, and garbage collection.

JRE (Java Runtime Environment)
The JRE is everything needed to run a Java program.
It includes the JVM and standard libraries, but does not include tools for writing or compiling Java code.

JDK (Java Development Kit)
The JDK is used to develop Java applications.
It contains the JRE plus developer tools like the Java compiler (javac) and debugger.
In short:
JDK = JRE + development tools


What is Bytecode?
When we write Java code (.java files), it is compiled by the Java compiler into bytecode (.class files).
Bytecode is an intermediate, platform-independent format that the JVM understands.
This bytecode can run on any system that has a compatible JVM installed.

Write Once, Run Anywhere ?
“Write once, run anywhere” means that Java code is written and compiled once, and the same compiled code can run on different operating systems without modification.
This is possible because Java programs run on the JVM, not directly on the operating system. As long as a JVM exists for a platform (Windows, Linux, macOS), the same Java bytecode will work across all of them.
